# Brutalism

Use intentionally raw composition, default-like controls, stark typography, and visible structure for experimental editorial experiences.

- **Palette:** Light background `#FFFFFF`, foreground `#000000`, primary `#0000EE`, accent `#FF2B00`, surface `#F1F1F1`, border `#000000`, focus `#FF00A8`. Dark background `#080808`, foreground `#FFFFFF`, primary `#7DB7FF`, accent `#FF775A`, surface `#171717`.
- **Type:** System sans or monospace; abrupt scale changes, uppercase utility labels, underlined links. Preserve comfortable body measure and line height.
- **Surfaces:** Square corners, exposed borders, plain blocks, rules, and almost no soft shadow. Asymmetry may be deliberate but reading and tab order remain logical.
- **Components:** Native-inspired buttons and fields with thick boundaries, raw tables, text-heavy cards, obvious alerts, direct navigation. Avoid ornamental component chrome.
- **Interaction:** Links visibly underline; hover may invert color or shift 1–2px. Focus uses a high-contrast ring, never an intentionally "broken" indicator. Disabled controls stay legible and use native semantics rather than looking accidentally broken.
- **Responsive:** Asymmetric desktop regions stack into a deliberate reading order. No clipped labels or novelty horizontal scrolling for core content.
- **Accessibility:** Raw aesthetics do not excuse invalid semantics, unpredictable navigation, flashing, or tiny controls. Preserve headings, landmarks, labels, and conventional error messages.
- **Theme/performance:** Dark mode retains stark contrast without pure-white long-form glare. Use native CSS and text; minimize imagery and motion, and honor reduced motion.

Representative preview: stark editorial landing page with exposed grid, oversized title, raw navigation, plain form, and structured content index.
